-- IMPL-`requires` DICT-PARAM ARITY CONFORMANCE (#1648 S1).
--
-- The regression guard that replaces `test/must_fail_fixtures/1648-impl-requires-
-- unused-dict-arity-skew-segfault/`, drained and deleted when #1648 was fixed.  It
-- keeps all cells of the issue's varied dimension -- does the impl method's
-- body READ the element dict its `requires` clause obligates? -- in one program, so
-- a regression cannot hide in the cell that has no observable.  CELL 4 adds the
-- second dimension the first three do not vary: is the reading body written
-- POINT-FREE (eta-short), so the clause carries the dict pattern but NO value
-- pattern at all?
--
-- The expected stdout in `impl_requires_dict_arity_conformance.build.golden` is
-- HAND-DERIVED from the language semantics, written down before either engine was
-- consulted ([WT-GOLDEN-ENSHRINES]); the interpreter agreed on all three cells
-- before AND during the defect, so a capture would have proven nothing.
--
-- The derivation, straight off the source:
--   valOnly  = 7   `Phantom`'s only field holds 7 and the arm is `Phantom n => n`.
--   noRead   = 22  the body is the literal 22; it reads neither dict nor value.
--   dictUser = 42  `if 1 == 1 then 41 + 1 else 0`.
--   dictUserPF = 55  `dictUserPF = selfEqPF`, so `selfEqPF (LivePF 2 54)`, which is
--                    `if 2 == 2 then 54 + 1 else 0`.
--
-- WHY IT CATCHES #1648.  dict_pass prepends an impl method's `$dict_<method>_<slot>`
-- param only when the body actually READS it (typecheck.mdk's `usesImplDict`), while
-- the call site stamps one route per DECLARED `requires` unconditionally.  Before the
-- fix, `valOnly`/`noRead` were DEFINED at arity 1 and CALLED with 2, so the single
-- surviving parameter bound positionally to the (null) dict slot:
--   * `valOnly` then matched a dictionary pointer against `Phantom` -> SIGSEGV
--     (exit 139, nothing printed) -- the pinned observable;
--   * `noRead` never reads the mis-bound word, so it printed 22 at exit 0 WITH THE
--     SKEW STILL PRESENT.  That cell is the reason this fixture exists: a fix
--     validated on the crashing cell alone looks correct and leaves this one wrong.
--   * `dictUser` declares the param (its body forces `Eq a`), so define and call
--     always agreed -- the arm that must not move.
-- Because all three now print through ONE golden line, a fix that re-elides the dict
-- at only one of them reds this gate rather than passing quietly.

-- CELL 1: body uses the VALUE but never the dict.  `a` is PHANTOM, so no body over
-- this type CAN force `Eq a` -- that is the shape, not an oversight.
data Phantom a = Phantom Int

interface ValOnly a where
  valOnly : a -> Int

impl ValOnly (Phantom a) requires Eq a where
  valOnly b = match b
    Phantom n => n

-- CELL 2: body reads NEITHER the dict nor the value -- the same skew with no
-- observable of its own.
data Blind a = Blind Int

interface NoRead a where
  noRead : a -> Int

impl NoRead (Blind a) requires Eq a where
  noRead _ = 22

-- CELL 3: the arity-UNCHANGED control -- the body USES the dict (`x == x` forces
-- `Eq a`), so the definition has always carried the leading dict param.  Its `a` is
-- a real field, which is the one extra dimension a live dict costs here.
data Live a = Live a Int

interface DictUser a where
  dictUser : a -> Int

impl DictUser (Live a) requires Eq a where
  dictUser (Live x n) = if x == x then n + 1 else 0

-- CELL 4: the POINT-FREE (eta-short) spelling of cell 3.  The body still reads the
-- dict -- `selfEqPF` forces `Eq a` internally -- so dict_pass DOES prepend the
-- `$dict_dictUserPF_0` param, but the clause carries NO value pattern, so its
-- pattern list is `[$dict_dictUserPF_0]` and `listLen pats - methodArity` reads
-- `1 - 1 = 0` leading dicts against a define that binds ONE.  The single value
-- argument then lands positionally in the dict slot -> SIGSEGV, exit 139, nothing
-- printed.  That is a DIFFERENT skew from cells 1-2 (there the define was short and
-- the CALL long; here the call is short and the DEFINE long), which is why cell 3
-- alone does not cover it: cell 3's clause has a value pattern and the subtraction
-- happens to agree there.  `implReqCount` counts the leading `$dict_*` patterns
-- directly now, so define and call agree for both spellings.
data LivePF a = LivePF a Int

interface DictUserPF a where
  dictUserPF : a -> Int

selfEqPF : Eq a => LivePF a -> Int
selfEqPF (LivePF x n) = if x == x then n + 1 else 0

impl DictUserPF (LivePF a) requires Eq a where
  dictUserPF = selfEqPF

main =
  println
    "valOnly=\{debug (valOnly (Phantom 7))} noRead=\{debug (noRead (Blind 7))} dictUser=\{debug (dictUser (Live 1 41))} dictUserPF=\{debug (dictUserPF (LivePF 2 54))}"
